of-symmetric-polynomials","used_for":"General-polynomial theorem.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-galois-group-embeds-in-the-symmetric-group-on-the-roots","used_for":"Low-degree solvability and general-polynomial theorem.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-ideal-correspondence-for-localisation","used_for":"Support under localisation.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-integers-modulo-n-basic-algebra","used_for":"Nakayama and local-isomorphism examples.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-legendre-symbol-multiplicativity","used_for":"Prime representation by x²+2y².","opened":true},{"id":"thm-localisation-at-a-prime-is-local","used_for":"Cyclic support, tensor support, and local examples.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-localisation-commutes-with-quotients","used_for":"Support of a cyclic quotient.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-localisation-equivalence-and-ring-laws","used_for":"Universal property, tensor description, and principal-neighbourhood vanishing.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-matrix-multiplication-laws","used_for":"Right action of SL₂(Z) on forms.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-normal-subgroups-and-quotients-in-the-galois-correspondence","used_for":"Kummer correspondence, roots-of-unity kernel, and radical solvability.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-orbit-stabilizer","used_for":"Prime-degree transitive subgroup theorem.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-proper-ideal-contained-in-maximal-ideal","used_for":"Local zero criteria and Jacobson radical characterization.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-purely-inseparable-extension-characterizations","used_for":"Norm/trace embedding formulas.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-right-exactness-of-tensor-products","used_for":"Quotients, exactness, and tensor support.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-second-supplement-to-quadratic-reciprocity","used_for":"Prime representation by x²+2y².","opened":true},{"id":"thm-separability-of-x-n-minus-one-and-the-order-of-the-group-of-roots-of-unity","used_for":"Cyclic Kummer theory and adjoining roots of unity.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-separable-degree-is-the-degree-of-the-separable-closure","used_for":"Norm/trace embedding formulas.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-sign-is-a-homomorphism","used_for":"Solvability of S_n for n≤4.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-simple-algebraic-extension-quotient-power-basis-and-degree","used_for":"Purely inseparable norm/trace example.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-solvable-iff-it-has-an-abelian-factor-series","used_for":"Solvability of S_n for n≤4.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-subgroups-and-quotients-of-solvable-groups-are-solvable","used_for":"Roots-of-unity kernel, radical solvability, and low-degree solvability.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-tensor-products-commute-with-arbitrary-direct-sums","used_for":"Localisation and arbitrary direct sums.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-tower-law-for-finite-field-extensions","used_for":"Tower laws for norm and trace.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-universal-property-of-module-direct-sums","used_for":"Finite intersections and Hom-localisation counterexample.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-universal-property-of-module-tensor-products","used_for":"Tensor description of module localisation.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-well-ordering-principle","used_for":"Termination of positive-definite reduction.","opened":true}],"concerns":[{"id":"thm-basic-laws-for-field-norm-and-trace","concern":"Step 1.3 asserts that the determinant of the restriction-of-scalars block matrix is N_{K/F}(det_K C). That is the substantive determinant identity needed for norm transitivity, but none of F1/L1/L3 states it and no derivation is supplied; the proof effectively asserts its hardest tower step.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"thm-kummer-correspondence","concern":"Step 1.4 says that a subgroup H maximal among those avoiding σ makes G/H a nontrivial simple abelian group. This is false: in G=C4 with σ the element of order 2, H={0} is maximal among subgroups avoiding σ, but G/H≅C4 is not simple. The character-separation argument needed to recover K is absent.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"thm-general-polynomial-of-degree-n-has-galois-group-s-n","concern":"Step 1.3 uses L2 to treat the multivariable field F(X1,…,Xn) and to conclude that every S_n-invariant rational function is a quotient of symmetric polynomials. The opened source cor-rational-function-field-as-a-fraction-field states only F(t)=Frac(F[t]) in one variable and does not give the invariant-field assertion.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"ex-cardano-from-the-lagrange-resolvent","concern":"The Example says u and v are the two nontrivial Lagrange resolvents. With def-lagrange-resolvent's normalization, the resolvents of the roots u+v, ζu+ζ²v, ζ²u+ζv are 3u and 3v, not u and v. The displayed radical root is correct, but the stated resolvent identification is false.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"ex-a-quartic-solved-by-its-resolvent-cubic","concern":"The title and Example claim the quartic is solved through the resolvent root y=2, but after computing the resolvent the proof simply sets z=x² and solves the biquadratic directly. No step derives x²=5±2√6 from the resolvent data, so the claimed method is not established.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"ex-an-artin-schreier-extension-over-f-p-of-t","concern":"Step 1.1 only proves t is not b^p−b. Step 2.1 then says L2 makes x^p−x−t irreducible, but the cited characterization assumes that polynomial is irreducible; it does not provide the missing Artin–Schreier irreducibility criterion. The proof is circular at the load-bearing step.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"ex-localised-hom-needs-finite-presentation","concern":"The item fixes S={p^n}, whose localisation of Z is Z[1/p], but the displayed comparison is written over Z_(p). The published convention def-localisation-at-a-prime-ideal makes Z_(p) the localisation at denominators prime to p, and 1/p is not in it. Thus the stated codomain and the witness φ(e_n)=1/p^n do not inhabit the same localisation.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"ex-localisation-does-not-commute-with-products","concern":"Again S={p^n}, so S^−1Z=Z[1/p], not Z_(p). The displayed target ∏Z_(p) does not contain the witness (1,1/p,1/p²,…). Replacing Z_(p) by Z[1/p] repairs the standard example, but as written its carrier is false.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"thm-field-norm-and-trace-by-embeddings","concern":"Step 1.2 identifies embeddings of K with extensions of embeddings of K_s. L4 gives uniqueness for the purely inseparable top extension but not existence of extensions of an F-embedding of K_s. The published algebraic embedding-extension theorem would close this missing citation.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"lem-normal-closure-of-a-radical-extension-is-radical","concern":"Step 2.2 identifies the normal closure with N(α_1,…,α_s,μ_m), which can be strictly too large; for the trivial step α=1 over Q with m=3 it adjoins μ_3 although the normal closure is Q. A local repair is to adjoin the actual conjugates of α, each still satisfying an m-th-power equation over N, without adjoining all μ_m.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"thm-cyclic-degree-n-extensions-with-roots-of-unity-are-kummer","concern":"Step 2.2 infers that α^n fixed by the full Galois group lies in F without citing the fixed-field clause. The opened thm-finite-galois-extension-characterizations does state K^Gal(K/F)=F, so adding the correct L4 citation closes the move.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"thm-localisation-of-modules-commutes-with-quotients-and-sums","concern":"Step 1.1 identifies the image of S^−1N→S^−1M with S^−1N before injectivity of localisation of N→M has been established. A direct fraction argument or the later injectivity lemma closes the identification.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"lem-localised-hom-finite-presentation-kernel-step","concern":"Step 1.2 cites a theorem stated only for short exact sequences to localise the general presentation R^m→R^n→M→0, where α need not be injective. Factoring through im α and using the short exact sequence 0→im α→R^n→M→0 closes the route.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"thm-support-under-localisation","concern":"L3 is an isomorphism for iterated ring localisations, while step 2.1 needs the corresponding module-localisation isomorphism (S^−1M)_q≅M_p. The fraction construction supplies it quickly, but the cited proposition as opened does not state it.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"ex-local-test-for-an-isomorphism","concern":"The prime-local check treats (2), (3), and every other prime (q), but omits the prime ideal (0) of Z. At (0), 6 becomes a unit and both sides localise to zero, so the omitted case closes immediately.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"def-trace-form-of-a-finite-extension","concern":"The Definition asserts bilinearity because trace is F-linear, but it cites only def-field-norm-and-trace, which defines trace as an operator trace and does not state F-linearity. The immediately preceding basic-laws theorem supplies the missing fact.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"fs-solvable-by-radicals-does-not-force-an-abelian-galois-group","concern":"Step 1.1 simply declares that the assignments on α and ζ_5 extend to automorphisms of Q(ζ_5,α). Verifying x^5−2 is irreducible and the degree-5 and degree-4 subextensions have trivial intersection supplies the short missing extension argument.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"ex-proper-versus-improper-equivalence-of-forms","concern":"Step 1.1 writes f|N for a determinant −1 matrix although def-proper-equivalence-of-binary-quadratic-forms defines that notation only for matrices in SL₂(Z). The substitution is clear, but the notation is formally outside its defined domain.","severity":"presentation"}],"alerts":[],"seams_checked":[]}